 ## Operating characteristics
-
-*   **Time Complexity**: $O(N \cdot T)$ where $N$ is the number of surfels and $T$ is the average number of surfels per tile. The tile-based approach reduces the complexity from $O(N \cdot H \cdot W)$ to $O(N + H \cdot W \cdot \text{avg\_surfels\_per\_tile})$ [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(N + H \cdot W)$ for storing surfel properties and output buffers [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance**: The method is optimized for GPU execution via fused kernels (e.g., Metal in the reference implementation). Performance scales with the number of surfels and image resolution. The tile-based approach ensures cache efficiency [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths**: Efficient rasterization of 2D surfels, support for spherical harmonics, differentiable projection (in the full pipeline) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations**: The reference implementation is non-differentiable (NumPy-based). The differentiable path uses a separate `accumulate` function. The method assumes planar surfels, which may not capture complex 3D geometry as well as 3D Gaussians in some cases [S1, unofficial].
